<?php

namespace Illuminate\Support\Traits;

/**
 * @template TKey of array-key
 * @template-covariant TValue
 */
trait EnumeratesValues
{
    // Same namespace as Illuminate\Conditionable\Traits\Conditionable's declared `namespace
    // Illuminate\Support\Traits;` (its directory doesn't match its namespace), so this is
    // unqualified in Laravel source too - copied verbatim.
    use Conditionable;

    /**
     * Create a new collection instance if the value isn't one already.
     *
     * Widened here (not on \Illuminate\Support\Collection) because a re-declaration on Collection
     * breaks return-type precision for DIRECT `Collection::make()` calls. Verified by a
     * controlled experiment (issue #808 round-2 review): moving this widened signature to
     * Collection.phpstub instead of the trait causes `Collection::make(null)`, `Collection::
     * make('str')`, `Collection::make($enumCase)`, and `Collection::make($plainDto)` to collapse
     * from their precise narrowed types (e.g. `Collection<0, 'str'>&static`) down to the stub's
     * widened-but-unbound fallback (`Collection<array-key, mixed>`, no `&static`) - because once
     * Collection self-declares `make()`, `declaring_method_id === method_id` for calls on
     * Collection itself, so `ExistingAtomicStaticCallAnalyzer` skips the declaring-class fallback
     * lookup entirely and only tries the called class (`Collection::class`, not registered - see
     * CollectionMakeHandler). Subclass calls (`EloquentCollection::make()`,
     * `WorkOrderCollection::make()`) were NOT affected by that experiment - they still dispatched
     * to the handler correctly with both the argument check and the return-type narrowing intact,
     * confirmed via direct handler instrumentation. Keeping the trait-level declaration avoids the
     * direct-call regression while keeping every dispatch path working.
     *
     * `scalar|UnitEnum|object` widens past Laravel's own `Arrayable|iterable|null` docblock because
     * `EnumeratesValues::getArrayableItems()` routes `null|scalar|UnitEnum` through `Arr::wrap()`
     * and any other object through `Arr::from()` (see issue #808). Precision for what
     * TMakeKey/TMakeValue resolve to in those cases comes from `CollectionMakeHandler`.
     *
     * @template TMakeKey of array-key
     * @template TMakeValue
     *
     * `...$args` is claimed unconditionally: it's real since Laravel 13.3.0 (now the 13.x floor
     * - see composer.json), and on the 12.x floor (12.20+) PHP silently ignores excess call-site
     * arguments beyond the native parameter list, so the wider claim is harmless there too.
     *
     * @param  \Illuminate\Contracts\Support\Arrayable<TMakeKey, TMakeValue>|iterable<TMakeKey, TMakeValue>|scalar|\UnitEnum|object|null  $items
     * @return static<TMakeKey, TMakeValue>
     */
    public static function make($items = [], ...$args) {}

    /**
     * Psalm drops Laravel's reflected `($callback is callable ? ...)` conditional, so `max()` and
     * `min()` fell back to `mixed`. `is null` and `is string` come first because the callable
     * branch first does not resolve. `TValue|null` is spelled out: the `?TValue` shorthand inside
     * a conditional makes Psalm return `mixed`. A string column stays `mixed` here; the Eloquent
     * collection handler narrows it when the column is a known model attribute.
     *
     * @template TMaxResult
     *
     * @param  (callable(TValue): TMaxResult)|string|null  $callback
     * @return ($callback is null ? TValue|null : ($callback is string ? mixed : TMaxResult|null))
     */
    public function max($callback = null) {}

    /**
     * @template TMinResult
     *
     * @param  (callable(TValue): TMinResult)|string|null  $callback
     * @return ($callback is null ? TValue|null : ($callback is string ? mixed : TMinResult|null))
     */
    public function min($callback = null) {}

    /**
     * Laravel returns `static<TFlatMapKey, TFlatMapValue>`, but a list-shaped callback result binds
     * the key to a literal (`0`, `int<0, max>`), which an invariant `Collection<int, V>` target
     * rejects. An int key is therefore widened to `int`; string keys survive. Larastan's
     * array-shape conditional does not bind its key template in Psalm.
     *
     * @template TFlatMapKey of array-key
     * @template TFlatMapValue
     *
     * @param  callable(TValue, TKey): (\Illuminate\Support\Collection<TFlatMapKey, TFlatMapValue>|array<TFlatMapKey, TFlatMapValue>)  $callback
     * @return static<(TFlatMapKey is int ? int : TFlatMapKey), TFlatMapValue>
     */
    public function flatMap(callable $callback) {}

    /**
     * Laravel returns `static<TKey, TValue>`, which leaks the unresolved class templates from a
     * static call; the decoded JSON has no known shape.
     *
     * @param  string  $json
     * @param  int  $depth
     * @param  int  $flags
     * @return static<array-key, mixed>
     */
    public static function fromJson($json, $depth = 512, $flags = 0, ...$args) {}

    /**
     * Laravel's `static<TUnwrapKey, TUnwrapValue>` parameter resolves to the unbound class
     * templates on a static call, and the invariant `Collection<array-key, mixed>` rejects any
     * concrete collection.
     *
     * @template TUnwrapKey of array-key
     * @template TUnwrapValue
     *
     * @param  array<TUnwrapKey, TUnwrapValue>|\Illuminate\Support\Enumerable<TUnwrapKey, TUnwrapValue>  $value
     * @return array<TUnwrapKey, TUnwrapValue>
     */
    public static function unwrap($value) {}
}
